<h2>The question</h2>

<p>
  A backlog item is stamped with a calendar day (<code>dateScaffolded: "2026-08-08"</code>). A staleness check
  later asks whether that day is <em>before today</em>. Both values are naked <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> strings
  with no time and no zone. When the stamp and the comparison are produced on machines in different time zones,
  the same instant yields two different "todays" and the check is wrong by a day.
</p>

<p>
  This topic grounds decision
  <a href="/backlog/2985-does-the-born-active-ttl-need-a-grace-day-for-operator-timez/">#2985</a> — does the
  <a href="/backlog/670-scaffold-new-items-as-active-until-settled-close-the-born-pu/">#670</a> born-active TTL
  need a grace day to absorb that skew? The general question behind it is reusable: <strong>where does the fix
  for a day-frame disagreement belong — in the datum, in the reader, or in the threshold?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Finding — the premise was already fixed</h2>

<p>
  The first result of the survey was a correction, not a design. Decision #2985 states that the ageing side
  reads UTC while the stamping side reads the operator's day. That was true of the tree <em>before</em> PR
  #1018 and is not true now: every clock read that feeds the TTL moved onto the operator-local helper in
  <a href="/backlog/2747-stamp-backlog-dates-in-the-operator-s-local-timezone-not-the/">#2747</a>'s own resolve
  commit.
</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Clock it reads</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>scripts/check-standards.mjs</code></td><td><code>localToday()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>scripts/check-backlog-workflow.mjs</code></td><td><code>localToday()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>scripts/audit-backlog-health.mjs</code> (the O1 flag)</td><td><code>localToday()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>scripts/backlog.mjs</code> → <code>scripts/backlog/scaffold.mjs</code> (the stamp)</td><td><code>localToday()</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>
  On any <em>single</em> machine the two frames are therefore identical and the skew is exactly zero. A second
  premise fell with it: the card assumed a fix needs "a shared operator-day helper reachable from the CJS
  invariants file." It does not. <code>scripts/lib/workflow-invariants.cjs</code> takes <code>today</code> as an
  <strong>injected</strong> argument and never reads a clock — deliberately, so the rule stays pure and
  fixture-testable. The frame is a property of the <em>caller</em>, and every caller is already an ES module.
</p>

<h2>Finding — what survives is a cross-<em>machine</em> gap</h2>

<p>
  "The operator's day" is defined by whichever host runs the check, and the repo health gate also runs in CI.
  Three GitHub Actions workflows invoke <code>npm run check:standards</code>; none of them sets
  <code>TZ</code> or <code>BACKLOG_TZ</code>, and GitHub-hosted runners are UTC. So in CI the check computes a
  UTC "today" and compares it against stamps written in the operator's day.
</p>

<p>
  The severity is low for a sharper reason than "it is only a warning": the false warning appears
  <em>only where nobody reads it</em>. The gate's exit code is driven by errors alone, the invariant pushes a
  warning, and the operator's own local run of the same gate is correct.
</p>

<h2>Prior art — how established systems frame a day</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Model</th><th>Lesson</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>TC39 <strong>Temporal</strong> (Stage 4, ES2026)</td>
      <td><code>PlainDate</code> is zone-independent by design; <code>Temporal.Now.plainDateISO(timeZone)</code>
        takes the zone as an argument. MDN's worked example shows one instant reading as
        <code>2021-10-01</code> with no argument and <code>2021-09-30</code> for <code>America/New_York</code>.</td>
      <td>There is no zone-free "today". The zone binds at the moment you ask.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>RFC 3339 / RFC 9557</strong></td>
      <td>3339 §4.3 spells "offset unknown" as <code>-00:00</code>; 9557 re-spells it <code>Z</code> and adds a
        bracketed IANA suffix — <code>2022-07-08T00:14:07+01:00[Europe/Paris]</code>.</td>
      <td>An offset is not a zone. Offsets move (DST, political redefinition), so calendar reasoning needs the
        <em>name</em>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Git</strong></td>
      <td>A commit stores <code>&lt;epoch seconds&gt; &lt;offset&gt;</code>. <code>--date=local</code> changes
        the rendered zone and never the stored seconds.</td>
      <td>Store the instant; carry the offset as a rendering hint only.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Noda Time</strong></td>
      <td><code>Instant</code> → <code>LocalDate</code> is always unambiguous;
        <code>LocalDate</code> → <code>Instant</code> is not.</td>
      <td>Comparing two naked local dates from different clocks sits on the ambiguous side of that asymmetry.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Staleness thresholds</strong> (dbt <code>warn_after</code>/<code>error_after</code> and
        neighbours)</td>
      <td>Buffers exist for <em>pipeline latency</em>, not for day framing.</td>
      <td>No citable source recommends padding a threshold to absorb timezone skew. Absence of evidence, not a
        prohibition — but a genuinely one-sided literature.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Two corrections the sources forced</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Temporal's documentation does <strong>not</strong> say a bare calendar date is meaningless without a zone.
    <code>PlainDate</code> is a deliberate concept for things that are correctly zone-independent — birthdays,
    recurring alarms. The zone becomes mandatory only when asking "what is today" or crossing to and from an
    instant. That is a narrower and more defensible claim than the one this survey started with.</li>
  <li>The GitHub contributions-graph story should <strong>not</strong> be cited as settled. GitHub's current
    docs state contributions are timestamped in UTC; GitHub's own 2014 announcement describes per-commit
    offset-aware bucketing. Two first-party sources, unreconciled — so it is not usable as precedent either
    way.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The rule this supports</h2>

<p><strong>Fix the frame, never the threshold.</strong> Two grounded reasons a grace day is the broken branch —
  which is why #2985 records it as a finding rather than offering it as a branch to choose:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>It does not make the clocks agree.</strong> It makes the check tolerate <em>any</em> one-day
    disagreement — including the genuine second-day staleness the TTL exists to catch. The signal it removes is
    the one it was protecting.</li>
  <li><strong>It pays globally for a local artifact.</strong> The laxity lands on every item on every machine,
    including the operator's own — where the frames already agree and where the warning is actually read.</li>
</ol>

<p>
  <strong>One reason was considered and withdrawn</strong>, and is named here rather than deleted so the
  rejection is not read as resting on it. An earlier draft argued a one-day pad "is not even sufficient",
  because two calendar frames can differ by <em>two</em> days — at UTC+14 it can be 2026-01-02 while at UTC−12
  it is 2025-12-31. True of the general case, but <em>inadmissible for this population</em>: the machines that
  actually compare these stamps are one operator's host in <code>America/Toronto</code> plus UTC-hosted
  runners, a maximum skew of one day. It is no longer offered as grounding.
</p>

<h2>Where the frame should be declared</h2>

<p>
  A per-reader pin already exists. <code>scripts/lib/local-date.mjs</code> documents <code>BACKLOG_TZ</code> as
  "an explicit IANA pin … for the operator whose HOST clock is already wrong, typically a UTC container" — which
  is precisely what a GitHub-hosted runner is. But it frames one reader at a time: it covers the hosts someone
  remembers to enroll, and no others. A devcontainer, a fresh clone in a UTC image, a second machine or a bot
  stays unframed, and each new workflow silently inherits today's wrong behaviour.
</p>

<p>
  So the recommendation is the <strong>repo-wide declaration</strong>: one tracked in-tree IANA zone name, read
  by <code>scripts/lib/local-date.mjs</code> as a rung <em>below</em> <code>BACKLOG_TZ</code>, so every reader
  <em>and</em> writer of the checkout shares one frame by construction and the per-host escape hatch keeps its
  precedence. That is #2985's Fork 1 default, (b).
</p>

<p>
  #2747 did deliberately reduce that ladder to <strong>one</strong> knob on review evidence — read at its actual
  scope, that evidence is that <code>TZ</code> is a POSIX variable, not an IANA name
  (<code>TZ=GMT+5</code> resolves to the zone <code>"+05:00"</code>, ten hours from the truth), so a
  <code>TZ</code> rung would let a non-IANA value frame a calendar day. <strong>That finding does not reach a
  tracked IANA declaration</strong>, which is validated against <code>Intl</code> and throws on a name
  <code>Intl</code> rejects — the very guarantee the finding was protecting. An earlier draft of this write-up
  claimed any second rung re-opens what #2747 closed; that was an over-reach beyond the evidence's scope, and
  the independent skeptic pass on #2985 corrected it.
</p>

